@@s001 Polynomial reductions
@@s002 ora:element of the reduction,ok:correspondence,dato:starting instance
@@s003 P2 = Vertex Cover: graph G and k. P1 = Sale: tiles with values; you buy the values at a cost. I want to prove <b>VC ≤p Sale</b>: if Sale is easy, so is Vertex Cover.
@@s004 I build P1 from G: <b>one tile per edge</b>. The edges 1–2 and 2–3 become the tiles (1,2) and (2,3). Polynomial time: one line per edge.
@@s005 Correspondence: buying the value 2 covers both tiles, that is, it is a vertex cover of size 1. The cost is k = 1 kr. Same answer: yes for the graph, yes for Sale.
@@s006 Polynomial reductions
@@s007 A reduction P2 ≤p P1 transforms every instance of P2 into an instance of P1 with the same answer, in polynomial time. If P1 were easy, P2 would be easy too: this is how one proves that P1 is NP-hard.
@@s008 The direction
@@s009 You want to prove that P1 is NP-hard. Take a problem P2 already known as NP-hard, and show <code>P2 ≤p P1</code>: an algorithm for P1 would give an algorithm for P2. The direction is <b>always from the known problem to the new one</b>. Not the other way round.
@@s010 Vertex cover reduces to Sale
@@s011 The six steps
@@s012 Choose P2 from the catalogue by looking at the structure. Do the special-case test: fix a parameter of P1 (for example <code>k = 2</code>).
@@s013 Decision version of P1 (yes/no on the size).
@@s014 <b>Construction</b>: "Given an instance of P2, we construct an instance of P1 as follows: …". Say how large it is.
@@s015 Polynomial time of the construction.
@@s016 <b>Correctness in both directions</b>: yes for P2 implies yes for P1, and vice versa.
@@s017 Complete small example: the instance of P2 and the constructed instance.
@@s018 Traps
@@s019 Do not prove that it is NP-hard starting from a solution: the construction starts from the instance of P2. Do not forget the direction <code>⇐</code>. Respect the input constraints of P1: if P1 promises something (for example strings all different), your instance must respect it.
